  "summary": "A sculptural headtie tradition of the Yoruba people featuring elaborate, architectural folds and dramatic shapes.",
  "description": "Gele creates bold, structured forms from fabric through intricate folding and wrapping techniques, often reaching dramatic heights. This tradition emerged from Yoruba cultural practices and has evolved to signify status, celebration, and cultural identity.",
  "culturalContext": "In the Ifa Literary Corpus, the Odu 'Ose-Otura' as read by Chief Ifa priest Solagbade Popoola, records the Gele as head gear in one of its verses;\n\n\"The Yoruba were among the first to wear headwraps as adornments. The idea spread, so that by the 1400s, headwraps of some kind were a firm part of many West African cultures.\"",
